WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.510
Last question today.

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:07.380
Hey David, do you believe that Medicare for
all should cover elective cosmetic and plastic

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.770
surgery?

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:14.360
So I'm going to answer the question as asked,
but then I'm going to tell you that there's

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.440
a major problem with this question.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:22.820
So during the debates, I often implore the
democratic candidates to immediately call

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.141
out questions is problematic when they are
rather than answering them as asked, this

00:00:27.141 --> 00:00:28.141
is my show.

00:00:28.141 --> 00:00:29.270
So I'll do it in the opposite way.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.800
I'm telling you the question is a problem,
but first I'll answer it as asked.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.600
The term elective is the complicating factor
here.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:45.569
It's pretty widely accepted that cheek implants
or breast implants for medically unnecessary,

00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:52.129
uh, elective cosmetic reasons would not be
covered by Medicare for all that, that you

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:54.159
find very little disagreement with that.

00:00:54.159 --> 00:01:00.210
Now, the counter argument would be, you know,
some of these things are medically unnecessary,

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.670
but someone's appearance is causing them emotional
distress.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:08.680
Their nose, uh, is causing them emotional
distress and they're in therapy because of

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.070
their nose and it should be covered for that
reason.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.760
It's not the most convincing argument to me,
but that is an argument that is sometimes

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:17.760
made.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.960
Then you get to, well what about cosmetic
surgery?

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.910
That's the result of a injury or illness.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.740
And most people say that that should be covered
by Medicare for all.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.690
And I completely agree.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:34.950
When you get to elective it can get pretty
complicated.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:40.530
There can be lots of things that are not medically
necessary as typically defined, but they're

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:41.890
not mere vanity.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.900
And the question then becomes where do we
draw the line at elective?

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.320
If you need breast reconstruction from breast
cancer, that's a definitive yes.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.560
If you want breast implants for vanity, typically
that's a definitive no.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.050
But what about something like breast lift
surgery for a post-pregnancy condition that

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:08.670
is not life threatening and it's a mix of
medically suggested and for physical appearance,

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:09.670
I don't know.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:15.030
I mean I lean to, it should be covered, but
these are the sort of middle of the road cases

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.319
that ended up becoming a debate.

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:18.519
But now, okay, put all of that aside.

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:19.659
Here's the real analysis.

00:02:19.659 --> 00:02:26.090
This is a complete red herring used to distract
from the reality that people already now are

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:33.549
foregoing tons of medically necessary and
preventive maintenance type procedures.

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:39.540
Getting things looked at before they become
serious because of the system that we have

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.530
and we already have private insurers making
decisions about what is and isn't covered.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:49.109
So the problem I have with this question about
whether or not elective cosmetic surgery would

00:02:49.109 --> 00:02:53.709
be covered under Medicare for all, is that
it's an attempt to attack Medicare for all

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:58.700
by saying either, wow, you would cover cosmetic
surgery.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.700
That's crazy.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.129
My taxes shouldn't pay for your nose job or
Oh, you won't cover it.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:07.290
Well, it's not really universal coverage is
it?

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.669
And this is the game we shouldn't be drawn
into.

00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:11.249
It's a complete distraction.

00:03:11.249 --> 00:03:14.840
I'm more than glad to have the conversation
here on the show because I'm answering your

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.510
questions and telling you what I think.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:21.879
But this is a complete and total distraction
from the fact that even if these questions

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:28.490
about cosmetic surgery remain countless, other
issues are resolved by Medicare for all, and

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:34.319
we already have the same type of issues that
people are describing about edge case, middle

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:37.189
of the road, elective cosmetic surgery.

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:42.400
So don't allow yourself to fall for what is
quite frankly, a distraction from the real

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.689
debate as to what kind of healthcare system
we should have in the United States that does

00:03:46.689 --> 00:03:48.099
it for questions today.

